Given an integer array `nums`, return `true` if any value appears at least twice.

```ts
function containsDuplicate(nums: number[]): boolean {
  const seen = new Set<number>();
  for (const num of nums) {
    if (seen.has(num)) {
      return true;
    }
    seen.add(num);
  }
  return false;
}
```

The naive answer compares every pair — O(n^2). A `Set` collapses that to a single pass: each value is checked against everything seen so far in O(1), so the whole scan is O(n) time at the cost of O(n) memory.

There is a third option worth knowing for interviews: sort first, then check adjacent pairs. That is O(n log n) time but only O(1) extra space — the classic time-for-memory trade.

Given two strings, decide whether `t` is a rearrangement of `s`.

```ts
function isAnagram(s: string, t: string): boolean {
  if (s.length !== t.length) {
    return false;
  }
  const counts = new Map<string, number>();
  for (const char of s) {
    counts.set(char, (counts.get(char) ?? 0) + 1);
  }
  for (const char of t) {
    const remaining = counts.get(char) ?? 0;
    if (remaining === 0) {
      return false;
    }
    counts.set(char, remaining - 1);
  }
  return true;
}
```

Count every character of `s`, then spend those counts down as you walk `t`. If `t` ever needs a character whose count has already hit zero, it is not an anagram.

The length guard at the top is doing real work: without it, `s = "aab"` and `t = "aa"` would drain the counts without ever hitting zero and wrongly return `true`. With equal lengths guaranteed, a single decrementing pass is enough — no second check that every count returned to zero.

Decide whether a string reads the same forwards and backwards, ignoring case and every non-alphanumeric character.

```ts
function isPalindrome(s: string): boolean {
  const cleaned = s.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]/g, '');
  let left = 0;
  let right = cleaned.length - 1;
  while (left < right) {
    if (cleaned[left] !== cleaned[right]) {
      return false;
    }
    left++;
    right--;
  }
  return true;
}
```

Two pointers walk inward from both ends, comparing as they go. The loop condition is `left < right`, not `left <= right`: once the pointers meet they are looking at the same character, and comparing it with itself proves nothing. On an odd-length string that middle character is simply never examined, which is correct — a single character is always a palindrome of itself.

This is O(n) time and, apart from the cleaned copy, O(1) working space.

Given the heads of two sorted linked lists, splice them into one sorted list.

```ts
function mergeTwoLists(a: ListNode | null, b: ListNode | null): ListNode | null {
  const dummy = new ListNode(0);
  let tail = dummy;
  while (a !== null && b !== null) {
    if (a.value <= b.value) {
      tail.next = a;
      a = a.next;
    } else {
      tail.next = b;
      b = b.next;
    }
    tail = tail.next;
  }
  tail.next = a ?? b;
  return dummy.next;
}
```

Two things make this short. First, the **dummy head**: without it every append would need an "is the result still empty?" branch to decide between setting the head and setting `tail.next`. The dummy gives `tail` something to point at from the very first iteration, and the real answer is just `dummy.next`.

Second, `tail.next = a ?? b` after the loop. The loop only exits when one list is exhausted, and whatever remains of the other is already sorted and already linked — so it gets attached wholesale rather than copied node by node.

Decide whether a linked list loops back on itself.

```ts
function hasCycle(head: ListNode | null): boolean {
  let slow = head;
  let fast = head;
  while (fast !== null && fast.next !== null) {
    slow = slow!.next;
    fast = fast.next.next;
    if (slow === fast) {
      return true;
    }
  }
  return false;
}
```

The obvious solution keeps a `Set` of visited nodes — correct, but O(n) memory. **Floyd's tortoise and hare** gets the same answer in O(1): move one pointer a single step and the other two steps per iteration. On a straight list the fast pointer simply runs off the end. Inside a cycle it cannot — it keeps looping, closing the gap on the slow pointer by exactly one node each iteration, so it is guaranteed to land on it eventually.

The loop condition checks both `fast` and `fast.next`, because the next line dereferences `fast.next.next` and either being null would throw.

Return the number of nodes along the longest path from the root down to a leaf.

```ts
function maxDepth(root: TreeNode | null): number {
  if (root === null) {
    return 0;
  }
  return 1 + Math.max(maxDepth(root.left), maxDepth(root.right));
}
```

Four lines, and every recursive tree problem looks like this: handle the empty case, then combine the answers from both subtrees.

Watch the base case though — it returns **0**, not -1. This problem counts *nodes*, so a single leaf has depth 1 and the empty tree has depth 0. That is a different convention from the classic `height()` function, which counts *edges* and therefore returns -1 for null and 0 for a leaf. The code is otherwise identical; only the base value moves. Interviewers ask which one you meant, so say it out loud.

The recursion visits every node once: O(n) time, and O(h) stack space where h is the height.

Shift every zero in `nums` to the end while keeping the order of the non-zero values, modifying the array in place.

```ts
function moveZeroes(nums: number[]): void {
  let insert = 0;
  for (const num of nums) {
    if (num !== 0) {
      nums[insert] = num;
      insert++;
    }
  }
  while (insert < nums.length) {
    nums[insert] = 0;
    insert++;
  }
}
```

Two passes, no extra array. The first compacts every non-zero value toward the front, with `insert` tracking where the next one belongs — it lags behind the read position by exactly the number of zeros seen so far. The second pass fills whatever remains with zeros.

The read pointer never falls behind `insert`, so overwriting `nums[insert]` mid-scan can never clobber a value still waiting to be read. Note the `void` return: the result *is* the mutation, and returning `nums` as well would be misleading about who owns the array.
